course: dictionary
lesson: 4049
title: Generalisation
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A generalisation is a statement about all, or most, things of one kind, based on some examples. «Turning one example into a rule is called a hasty generalisation.» The word rule is a clue: a generalisation takes a few cases and stretches them to cover many.
explain: Generalisations can be useful. In maths, you look at a pattern like «each term is 3 more than the last» and make a rule that works for every term. In science, you test things with experiments and then draw a conclusion. The key question is: do the examples really support the rule?
explain: A sweeping generalisation is too broad and unfair. «Judging a whole group by a few of its members is a hasty generalisation.» If you meet two rude children from one school and decide everyone there is rude, you have judged a whole group from too little evidence. That is unfair to the people you have not met.
fact: A generalisation is a statement about all or most things of a kind.
fact: A generalisation is based on some examples.
fact: A sweeping generalisation is too broad and unfair, like «all boys hate reading».
fact: A hasty generalisation is a rule made from too few examples: «one time tells you very little».
fact: In maths, a generalisation is a rule that works for all cases, like «the sum of two even numbers is always even».
exercise: «Judging a whole group by a few of its members is a hasty generalisation.» What is a hasty generalisation? | *Judging a whole group from only a few of its members | A rule in maths that is true for all cases | A question that has no answer | Counting every member of a group carefully
exercise: Mia says, «All boys hate reading.» What kind of generalisation is this? | *A sweeping generalisation, too broad and unfair | A conclusion from a careful experiment | A rule that works for all cases in maths | A fact about all boys
exercise: In maths you notice, «each term is 3 more than the last». What should you make from this pattern? | A guess that only fits the first term | *A rule that works for every term | A list of every number in the world | A question about the weather
test: «The sum of two even numbers is always even.» Why is this a generalisation? | It is a story about a group of children | *It is a rule that works for all cases | It is a question with no answer | It is a single example of one number
test: Which sentence is a sweeping generalisation? | *«All teachers are strict.» | «Two of my friends like football.» | «The bus was late today.» | «Lemons taste sour.»
exam: Why is judging everyone in a group from one example unfair? | *Because one example tells you very little about the whole group | Because groups never have members | Because it takes too long to count | Because examples are always wrong
